The trait is the seam for a `MetricsCapacity` implementation that reads live signals from the inference backend:

```rust
// planned, not yet implemented
pub struct MetricsCapacity {
    aibrix_client: AibrixMetricsClient,
}

impl CapacityProvider for MetricsCapacity {
    fn max_in_flight(&self) -> usize {
        // Read Aibrix queue depth + KV-cache utilization
        // Return a dynamic limit that shrinks when the backend is under pressure
        self.compute_safe_limit()
    }
}
```

Swapping in a `MetricsCapacity` changes only the `CapacityProvider` binding — the fairshare scheduler and the entire rest of the pipeline are unchanged. This is by design.

```rust
pub trait CapacityProvider: Send + Sync + 'static {
    /// Maximum number of requests allowed in flight right now.
    fn max_in_flight(&self) -> usize;
}
```

The fairshare scheduler calls this every time it considers granting a permit. The result is the global concurrency ceiling.

The `Tokenizer` trait in `obleth-tokenizer` is the seam:

```rust
pub trait Tokenizer: Send + Sync {
    fn count_text(&self, text: &str) -> u32;
    fn estimate_request(&self, body: &Value) -> CostEstimate { ... }
}
```

Drop in a `tiktoken-rs` or HuggingFace `tokenizers` implementation for model-accurate counting without changing anything else in the pipeline.

Serve a JSON document, unauthenticated, at a stable URL. Every key needs a
`kid`, because the gateway looks keys up by `kid` alone and silently skips
entries without one.

```json
{
  "keys": [
    {
      "kty": "EC",
      "crv": "P-256",
      "alg": "ES256",
      "use": "sig",
      "kid": "2026-09-a",
      "x": "…base64url…",
      "y": "…base64url…"
    }
  ]
}
```

Node can derive the `x`/`y` pair from the public PEM directly, which avoids
transcribing them by hand:

```js
import { createPublicKey } from "node:crypto";
import { readFileSync } from "node:fs";

const jwk = createPublicKey(readFileSync("signing-key.pub.pem")).export({
  format: "jwk",
});
console.log(JSON.stringify({ ...jwk, alg: "ES256", use: "sig", kid: "2026-09-a" }));
```

Any stable string works as a `kid` as long as it changes when the key does; a
date-stamped label is enough. This endpoint is public by design — it contains
only public keys, and the gateway fetches it without credentials.

